96-## A11.4 Dependency duty for registered decisions
97-
98-When a registered decision changes a parameter (an algorithm, a custody form, a channel, a horizon, a scope, a key),
99-the owner sweeps the registered texts for sentences whose referent the change alters. Each one is recorded as
100-amended, superseded, still fitting or open, before the decision's change is treated as complete. An owner decision
101-that touches a registered parameter is checked against those texts before it is recorded.
102-
103-## A11.5 Results of the first sweep (2026-10-28)
104-
105-The sweep covered the original, Amendments 1–10, the freeze, the tracker and the author-rulings files
106-(`docs/reviews/stale-dependencies/SWEEP-2026-10-28.md`). The owner checked each finding against its source. The
107:review added item 3.
108-
109-1. **H3 (`phase-0-prereg.md:232-248`): not amended.** H3 is a hypothesis with a stated falsifier. Its examples are
110:   AWS-based, but they do not restrict it to AWS. It is measured over the whole deployed system, every provider
111-   included. A measurement protocol is recorded before measurement. It covers retained resources (including the
112-   compliance-locked trail bucket), defines the demonstration and idle periods, and reports the three numbers
113-   separately. A slot-2 signer and an issuer poller that run through the operating window conflict with "idle
114-   compute exactly $0". If that or another number fails, H3 is reported as falsified. The operating window is not
115-   redefined as a demonstration to avoid this.
116-2. **The slot-2 enforcement boundary: for the author's ruling.** A9.6 requires the final custody implementation to
117-   be deadline-qualified (`phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:389-392`). The frozen record keeps "the selected,
118-   deadline-qualified custody implementation" (`270ca49:docs/V1-PROFILE.md:1023-1026`) and describes it through
119-   `prototype/policy-signer/`, which met the deadline on 2026-10-14. That qualification stands as historical
120-   evidence. It does not qualify a replacement. The E9 ruling R5b (`docs/reviews/e9-packet/AUTHOR-RULINGS.md:54`,
121-   packet row 5b) allows porting instrument code with evidence earned on the resulting bytes. But it does not
122-   address A9.6's deadline-qualified custody, so it does not settle this question. Keeping the hybrid **with the
123-   replacement signer** depends on this ruling. Keeping it with the prototype does not.
124-   **Proposed:** `service/signer-gcp/` may replace the prototype, provided that before the first shipped receipt:
125-   - (i) it preserves **every A9.6 custody and policy requirement**: complete-frame validation; the declared-time
126-     check on its own clock before signing; the issuing account able to enqueue but not to invoke the key, change
127-     the policy or clock, or obtain key-use credentials; and the key dedicated to V1 type-0x00 documents. It also
128-     preserves every property in the frozen record;
129:   - (ii) the external custody record names the deployed implementation, its image digest and its configuration;
130:   - (iii) deployed evidence is earned on the new bytes. That means: a deployed positive signature that verifies
131:     offline; deployed refusals for a malformed frame, a wrong tag, a wrong alg, a wrong fingerprint and a declared
132-     time outside ε; a review of every principal that can cause a signature or administer the signer
133-     (`service/issuer/DEPLOY.md` step 8); and verified retirement of every other signing path (step 7);
134-   - (iv) all of it identifies the tested image and configuration.
135-
136-   **Without this ruling**, production uses the deadline-qualified prototype signer. Slot 2 then has no signer-side
137-   signing stop or `sign_by` (`prototype/policy-signer/signer/main.py`), and frames travel inline in SQS messages,
138-   which cannot carry the 1 MiB client limit (IS-09, `docs/reviews/issuer/CODEX-REVIEW.md:35`). That limit is an
139-   owner operational resolution, stamped but not registered (`docs/V1-SERVICE-RESOLUTIONS.md:20`). It would be
140-   narrowed.
141:3. **The spike keys (`formal/p8/HYBRID-SUPPLEMENT.md` §11 item 6, lines 524-526): unchanged.** "Both spike keys
142-   are deleted after the re-capture verifies, once the review clears." The owner's deployment draft
143-   (2026-10-27) reused them as production keys. That contradicted this commitment, and the draft is withdrawn.
144-   Production uses new keys (`service/issuer/DEPLOY.md`), recorded in the external custody record before use.
145-4. **A dangling cross-reference (`phase-0-prereg-amendment-10.md:675`).** Row 25 was cut (E9 row 2,
146-   2026-10-23; profile §5). This is a CLARIFY: the fallback cites row 15 only.
147-5. **K-R's enforcement (`formal/p8/HYBRID-SUPPLEMENT.md:148-172, :533`): amended in wording, the assumption
148-   unchanged.** (K-R) itself stands: the ML-DSA-65 key signs only V1 tagged strings, in pure mode with the empty
149-   context, under EXTERNAL_MU, for no other protocol. What changes is who enforces it. The supplement names the
150-   issuer as the computer of `μ` and the issuer role as the only `kms:Sign` principal. In V1 as designed (deployment pending), a
151-   dedicated policy signer validates the complete frame (tag, alg, key fingerprint, ε, `sign_by`, the signing stop)
152-   *before* computing `μ`, and its role is the only `kms:Sign` principal (`service/signer-kms/`; key policy in
153-   `service/signer-kms/infra/main.tf`). This narrows what a captured issuer role alone can get signed. It does not
154-   change the supplement's stated limit: "whoever may call `kms:Sign` on the key can sign any message by computing
155:   `μ`". An administrator of the account can still grant that (`PutKeyPolicy`), which is C3.
